<header class="lesson">
  <div class="crumb">Lesson 0015 &middot; cpp_games / docs / learning</div>
  <h1>Debugging runtime spikes</h1>
  <p class="why">Why this matters: lessons 0003, 0005, 0009, and 0014 all ended with "measure before you change" &mdash; this is the lesson where you finally get the measuring kit. A game that averages 60 FPS but hitches for 80 ms once a second <em>feels broken</em>; a game that averages 45 FPS steadily feels fine. Spikes are a different beast from slowness, and they're hunted differently.</p>
</header>

<h2>1. The concept &mdash; spikes are not slowness</h2>

<p>Two performance diseases, two different treatments:</p>

<table class="compare">
  <tr><th></th><th>Slow (chronic)</th><th>Spiky (acute)</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Symptom</td><td>Every frame takes too long; FPS uniformly low</td><td>Most frames are fine; occasional frames take 3&ndash;30&times; longer</td></tr>
  <tr><td>What the player feels</td><td>Sluggish but consistent</td><td>Stutter, hitching, "the game froze for a moment"</td></tr>
  <tr><td>What reveals it</td><td>Average frame time</td><td><b>Worst frame</b> / 99th percentile &mdash; the average actively hides it</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Typical cause</td><td>Too much work per frame (algorithmic)</td><td>Rare-event work landing on one frame: allocation storms, lock waits, blocking I/O, OS preemption</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Typical fix</td><td>Do less work / better algorithm</td><td>Move the rare event off the hot path, amortize it, or pre-do it</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="callout">
  <h3>Why the average lies</h3>
  <p>1000 frames: 999 take 10 ms, one takes 100 ms. Average = 10.09 ms &mdash; "smooth 99 FPS!" But the player saw a visible hitch. The only numbers that catch it: <b>max</b> (100 ms) and <b>p99</b> (the time the worst 1% of frames exceed). Rule: <em>report frame times as worst/p99/median, never as average FPS</em>. This is lesson 0003's latency-vs-throughput distinction with teeth.</p>
</div>

<h3>The four-step spike hunt</h3>

<div class="step-diagram">  1. CONFIRM    Record per-frame times into a ring buffer. Is there a spike
                at all, how big, how often? (No tooling yet — 20 lines of code.)
  2. LOCATE     Split the frame into sections (events / update / render /
                display). Time each. When a spike frame happens, which
                section ate the budget?
  3. CLASSIFY   Match the guilty section against the usual-suspects table
                (section 4): allocation? lock? syscall? GPU? OS?
  4. FIX + RE-MEASURE
                Apply the matching fix, then rerun step 1. If the worst
                frame didn't improve, revert — the suspicion was wrong.</div>

<p>The discipline matters more than the tools: <b>never skip from symptom to fix</b>. Every lesson so far that said "profile first" was protecting you from optimizing the wrong thing — lesson 0014's <code class="inline">wrap()</code> suspicion is exactly the kind of claim step 2 confirms or kills.</p>

<h2>2. Minimal example &mdash; a 30-line spike detector</h2>

<p>This is self-contained instrumentation you can paste into any of your three games today. Two pieces: the right clock, and spike-triggered logging.</p>

<pre class="code"><span class="pp">#include</span> <span class="str">&lt;chrono&gt;</span>
<span class="pp">#include</span> <span class="str">&lt;cstdio&gt;</span>

<span class="com">// ALWAYS steady_clock for intervals. system_clock is wall-clock time and</span>
<span class="com">// can jump (NTP sync, DST) — a "frame" could measure negative.</span>
<span class="kw">using</span> Clock = std::chrono::steady_clock;

<span class="kw">struct</span> SectionTimer {                       <span class="com">// RAII: times a scope (lesson 0006)</span>
    Clock::time_point start = Clock::now();
    <span class="typ">double</span>&amp; sink;                          <span class="com">// where to record milliseconds</span>
    <span class="kw">explicit</span> SectionTimer(<span class="typ">double</span>&amp; s) : sink(s) {}
    ~SectionTimer() {
        sink = std::chrono::duration&lt;<span class="typ">double</span>, std::milli&gt;(Clock::now() - start).count();
    }
};

<span class="com">// In the game loop:</span>
<span class="typ">double</span> msUpdate = <span class="num">0</span>, msRender = <span class="num">0</span>;
<span class="kw">auto</span> frameStart = Clock::now();

{ SectionTimer t(msUpdate);  updateEverything(dt); }   <span class="com">// destructor records</span>
{ SectionTimer t(msRender);  renderScene(window);  }

<span class="typ">double</span> msFrame = std::chrono::duration&lt;<span class="typ">double</span>, std::milli&gt;(Clock::now() - frameStart).count();

<span class="com">// SPIKE-TRIGGERED logging: only speak when something is wrong.</span>
<span class="com">// Logging every frame would itself allocate/IO and distort the measurement</span>
<span class="com">// (the observer effect). 25 ms = 1.5x the 60 Hz budget.</span>
<span class="kw">if</span> (msFrame &gt; <span class="num">25.0</span>) {
    std::printf(<span class="str">"SPIKE %.1fms (update %.1f, render %.1f)\n"</span>,
                msFrame, msUpdate, msRender);
}</pre>

<ul>
  <li><b><code class="inline">steady_clock</code>, never <code class="inline">system_clock</code></b> &mdash; steady is monotonic (only moves forward, at a constant rate). System clock is "what time is it," adjustable by the OS at any moment.</li>
  <li><b>RAII timer</b> &mdash; the destructor records the time, so early returns and exceptions are timed correctly for free. Same pattern as <code class="inline">lock_guard</code> (lesson 0005).</li>
  <li><b>Spike-triggered output</b> &mdash; the conditional <code class="inline">printf</code> runs ~never, so the instrumentation's own cost stays out of the numbers. This matters: instrumentation that's heavier than the thing measured produces fiction.</li>
  <li><b>Measure in release</b> (<code class="inline">-O2</code>, <code class="inline">CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release</code>) &mdash; debug builds are 5&ndash;30&times; slower in different *proportions* per function, so debug-build spikes often point at the wrong suspect entirely.</li>
</ul>

<h2>3. In this project</h2>

<h3>The spike defense you already shipped</h3>
<small class="path">cpp_shooter_game/src/main.cpp:1093</small>
<pre class="code"><span class="kw">const</span> <span class="typ">float</span> dt = std::min(<span class="num">0.03f</span>, frameClock.restart().asSeconds());</pre>
<p>This clamp says: "even if the last frame took 200 ms, pretend at most 30 ms passed." Without it, a single spike would feed a huge <code class="inline">dt</code> into the physics &mdash; projectiles tunneling through enemies, the player launched by one integration step. Understand what this is: <b>mitigation, not cure</b>. It stops a spike from corrupting the simulation, but the player still <em>sees</em> the hitch. (Side effect worth knowing: during a spike the game world runs slower than real time, because clamped dt &lt; real elapsed time.)</p>

<h3>A real suspect hiding in the loop</h3>
<small class="path">cpp_shooter_game/src/main.cpp:1108</small>
<pre class="code"><span class="bad">window.setTitle(buildTitle(game));   <span class="com">// EVERY frame</span></span></pre>
<p><code class="inline">setTitle</code> isn't a normal function call &mdash; on Linux it's a round-trip conversation with the X11/Wayland window manager (syscalls + IPC to another process, lesson 0009), and <code class="inline">buildTitle</code> constructs a fresh <code class="inline">std::string</code> first (allocation, lesson 0003). At 60 Hz that's 60 window-manager chats per second to display mostly-unchanged text. Whether it actually spikes depends on the WM &mdash; <em>which is precisely what step 2 instrumentation would tell you</em>. The fix shape, if guilty: update the title only when the text changes, or every 30 frames.</p>

<h3>The two long-standing suspicions this lesson can close</h3>
<small class="path">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs/src/DialogUI.cpp:109+ &middot; src/LlmClient.cpp:62</small>
<p><b>(a)</b> Lesson 0003 flagged <code class="inline">DialogUI::render()</code>: it re-wraps and re-allocates transcript strings every frame. Wrap <code class="inline">ui.render(window)</code> in a <code class="inline">SectionTimer</code> and watch what happens as the transcript grows to 200 lines &mdash; that's the experiment, ~4 lines of code. <b>(b)</b> The main thread's <code class="inline">drainReplies()</code> takes a mutex the worker also takes. Could the worker make the main thread wait? Look at the design: the worker holds <code class="inline">replyMutex_</code> only to <code class="inline">push_back</code> one element (microseconds) &mdash; the long HTTP call happens <em>outside</em> any lock, and <code class="inline">drainReplies</code> uses <code class="inline">swap</code> to make its critical section tiny too. This was lesson 0005's design paying off: <b>the slow operation was kept outside the lock specifically so it can't become a main-thread spike</b>. Instrumentation should confirm near-zero wait — if it doesn't, something changed.</p>

<h3>What 60 FPS limiting actually does</h3>
<small class="path">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs/src/main.cpp:121 &middot; cpp_racing_game/src/main.cpp:1018</small>
<pre class="code">window.setFramerateLimit(<span class="num">60</span>);</pre>
<p>SFML implements this by <em>sleeping</em> the remainder of each frame. Sleeps are handled by the OS scheduler (lesson 0009) with ~1 ms granularity, so frame pacing under a sleep-based limiter is inherently a little jittery, and a "17.4 ms frame" in your logs may just be sleep overshoot &mdash; not your code. Know your instrument: measure the <em>work</em> portion (before the implicit sleep in <code class="inline">display()</code>) separately from the whole frame, or your spike log fills with false positives.</p>

<h2>4. The usual suspects — classify the guilty section</h2>

<table class="compare">
  <tr><th>Cause</th><th>Signature</th><th>Fix shape</th></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Heap allocation in hot path</b> (lesson 0003/0006)</td><td>Small frequent spikes; worse as data grows (transcript, projectiles)</td><td>Reuse buffers, <code>reserve()</code>, move work out of the loop</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Vector reallocation</b> (lesson 0002)</td><td>One spike exactly when a vector crosses a capacity boundary, then quiet</td><td><code>reserve()</code> the realistic maximum up front</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Lock contention</b> (lesson 0005)</td><td>Spikes only while the other thread is busy (e.g. during an LLM reply)</td><td>Shrink critical sections; never hold a lock across slow work</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Blocking syscall / I/O on main thread</b> (lesson 0009)</td><td>Spike correlated with a file read, font load, network call, <code>setTitle</code></td><td>Do it once at startup, cache it, or move to a worker thread</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>OS preemption / other processes</b></td><td>Random, uncorrelated with your code; worse when the machine is busy</td><td>Mostly accept it; close background hogs; (extreme: thread priority)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>First-use / cold-start costs</b></td><td>Spikes only the first time something happens (first shot fired, first reply)</td><td>Pre-warm during loading: touch the code path once before play starts</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>GPU / vsync stall</b></td><td><code>display()</code> section spikes while CPU sections stay flat</td><td>Reduce draw cost; check vsync vs framerate-limit interaction</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="callout warn">
  <h3>One spike cause lives outside your process</h3>
  <p>Your game shares the machine with Ollama. When the model is generating, the GPU and several CPU cores are saturated &mdash; and your game's frame times <em>will</em> degrade during replies even though no line of your code changed. Instrumentation that logs "spike during companion reply" is reporting a real phenomenon: a sidecar process (lesson 0001) competing for hardware. The fix is capacity planning (smaller model, limited threads), not C++.</p>
</div>

<h2>5. When in-code timers aren't enough — the tool ladder</h2>

<ul>
  <li><b>Rung 1: what you built in section 2.</b> Frame ring buffer + section timers + spike-triggered logs. Zero dependencies, answers "is there a spike and which quarter of the frame owns it." Start here, stay here as long as it keeps answering questions.</li>
  <li><b>Rung 2: <code class="inline">perf</code> (Linux).</b> A <em>sampling</em> profiler: interrupts your process thousands of times/sec and records where it was. <code class="inline">perf record ./shooter</code> &rarr; <code class="inline">perf report</code> shows which functions eat CPU overall. Great for chronic slowness; weaker for rare spikes (a 1-in-1000 frame contributes few samples).</li>
  <li><b>Rung 3: Tracy.</b> The game-industry standard frame profiler: an <em>instrumentation</em> profiler with a live timeline UI &mdash; you see every frame as a bar, click the tall one, and read exactly which zones ran. It's a header + macros (<code class="inline">ZoneScoped</code>) and would slot into your CMake like httplib did. The natural upgrade once rung 1 says "render() spikes" and you need to know <em>which part</em> of render.</li>
</ul>

<h2>6. Often confused</h2>

<h3><code>steady_clock</code> vs <code>system_clock</code></h3>
<p><b>Share:</b> both are <code class="inline">std::chrono</code> clocks returning <code class="inline">time_point</code>s. <b>Diverge:</b> <code class="inline">system_clock</code> tracks wall-clock time and may jump backward/forward when the OS adjusts time (NTP, timezone); <code class="inline">steady_clock</code> is monotonic &mdash; meaningless as a date, perfect as a stopwatch. <b>Pick:</b> durations/intervals &rarr; steady; timestamps for humans &rarr; system. (SFML's <code class="inline">sf::Clock</code> is monotonic too &mdash; your games' <code class="inline">frameClock</code> is already the right kind.)</p>

<h3>Sampling vs instrumentation profilers</h3>
<p><b>Share:</b> both tell you where time goes. <b>Diverge:</b> sampling (perf) statistically interrupts &mdash; near-zero setup, low overhead, but rare events hide between samples; instrumentation (Tracy, your SectionTimer) records every entry/exit of marked zones &mdash; exact and spike-friendly, but only sees what you marked, and each marker costs a little. <b>Pick:</b> chronic slowness &rarr; sampling; spikes &rarr; instrumentation. They complement; pros run both.</p>

<h3>Profiler vs debugger</h3>
<p><b>Share:</b> both inspect a live program. <b>Diverge:</b> a debugger (gdb) answers "what is the program doing / why is state wrong" by pausing it; a profiler answers "where does time go" by watching it run at speed. Pausing destroys timing &mdash; you can't debug a spike with breakpoints, because the breakpoint <em>is</em> a million-times-bigger spike.</p>

<h3>Spike vs slow (one more time, as a reflex)</h3>
<p>Before touching code, classify: is the worst frame an outlier (spike &rarr; find the rare event) or is the median frame already over budget (slow &rarr; reduce per-frame work)? The fixes don't transfer. <code class="inline">reserve()</code> won't fix a slow algorithm; a better algorithm won't fix an allocation storm that hits one frame in 500.</p>

<h2>7. Quiz</h2>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">1. Your spike log uses <code>system_clock</code> and one night reports a frame that took <b>-3,600,000 ms</b>. What happened?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Integer overflow produces garbage values, but -3,600,000 ms is suspiciously exactly negative one hour — that's a clock adjustment, not arithmetic.">A signed integer overflow in the duration math</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Exactly — system_clock is wall-clock time, and the OS adjusted it (DST ended / NTP correction) mid-frame, so 'end minus start' went negative by precisely an hour. steady_clock is monotonic and can never do this. That's why intervals always use steady_clock.">The OS moved the wall clock back an hour mid-frame; system_clock followed it</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="A real stall makes a hugely POSITIVE measurement, never negative — time still moved forward.">The game genuinely stalled for negative time due to a scheduler bug</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="duration<double, milli> handles unit conversion correctly; mixing units gives wrong magnitudes, not an exact negative hour.">Mixing milliseconds and microseconds in the conversion</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">2. The game averages 60 FPS but players report stutter. Which single number is most likely to expose the problem?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The average is exactly the number that's hiding the problem — 999 good frames drown one terrible frame.">Average frame time over the session</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Total CPU usage tells you the machine isn't saturated, but a spike can happen at 20% average CPU — it's about one frame, not the aggregate.">Overall CPU utilization percentage</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right — worst frame (or p99) is where spikes live. A 100 ms max against a 16.7 ms median says 'stutter' even when the average looks perfect.">Maximum (or 99th-percentile) frame time</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Median is even more spike-blind than the average — it ignores outliers by construction.">Median frame time</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">3. Why does the section-2 detector only <code>printf</code> when a frame exceeds 25 ms, instead of logging every frame?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Disk space is a side benefit, not the reason — a session's frame log is a few MB at most.">To save disk space over long play sessions</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct — printf does formatting and I/O (a syscall, lesson 0009). Doing that 60×/sec adds cost to every frame and can CAUSE the spikes you're hunting. Conditional logging keeps the instrument's cost out of the measurement — the observer effect, managed.">So the instrumentation itself doesn't distort the timing it's measuring</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="printf is thread-safe per call in practice — and this runs on one thread anyway. The concern is cost, not safety.">Because printf isn't thread-safe inside a game loop</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The threshold isn't about correctness of the numbers — sub-25 ms frames are measured fine; they're just not worth reporting.">Frames under 25 ms can't be measured accurately</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">4. Instrumentation shows the NPC game's frame times degrade only while the companion is generating a reply, even though <code>drainReplies()</code> shows ~0 ms lock wait. Best explanation?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The measurement just told you the lock wait is ~0 — believing the suspicion over the measurement is the exact anti-pattern this lesson exists to break.">The mutex contention is real but too fast for steady_clock to see</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right — Ollama is a separate process saturating the GPU and CPU cores during generation (lesson 0009: shared machine, OS scheduler arbitrates). Your code didn't get slower; the hardware got busier. Fix is capacity planning, not C++.">The Ollama sidecar process is competing for CPU/GPU while generating</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The worker thread spends its time BLOCKED in the HTTP call (waiting on recv — lesson 0009), which uses no CPU. One mostly-blocked thread doesn't starve a modern multi-core machine.">The LlmClient worker thread is hogging the core the game needs</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The reply only enters DialogUI after it arrives — during generation the transcript isn't growing yet, so render cost is unchanged.">DialogUI::render gets slower because the transcript is growing during generation</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">5. The shooter's <code>dt = std::min(0.03f, frameClock.restart().asSeconds())</code> clamp — what does it actually do about spikes?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="It changes nothing about how long frames take — it only changes what the simulation is TOLD about elapsed time.">It prevents frames from taking longer than 30 ms</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Backwards — it makes the simulation run SLOWER than real time during a spike (less simulated time passes than wall time).">It speeds the simulation up to catch up after a hitch</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Exactly — it's damage control, not prevention. The hitch is still visible to the player, but the physics never integrates a giant dt (which would tunnel projectiles through enemies). Mitigation ≠ cure; you still have to hunt the spike.">It stops a spike from corrupting the physics, but the visible hitch remains</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The clamp doesn't measure or report anything — it silently absorbs the anomaly, which is arguably why the spike was never investigated.">It logs oversized frames so spikes can be diagnosed later</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

